response:
 Since the larger of the two circles is tangent to the $x$-axis and the two circles are symmetric around the origin, the center of the larger circle is on the $x$-axis. Thus, the center of the larger circle is at $\left(\sqrt{6},0\right),$ or $\left(-\sqrt{6},0\right).$ Therefore, the equation of the circle centered at $\left(\sqrt{6},0\right)$ is
\[\left(x - \sqrt{6}\right)^2 + y^2 = r^2.\]We can assume that the ellipse and the circle intersect at the point where $x = \sqrt{6},$ as shown: [asy]
unitsize(0.04 cm);

real upperparab(real x) {
  return(6/(5 + abs(x)^2));
}

trig_axes(real x0, real x1, real y0, real y1, bool upper=true, bool lower=true, pen color=black) {
  draw((x0,0)--(x1,0),color+1bp,Arrow(6));
  draw((0,y0)--(0,y1),color+1bp,Arrow(6));
  if (upper) { draw(graph(upperparab, x0, x1),color+1bp);}
  if (lower) { draw(graph(lowerparab, x0, x1),color+1bp);}
  if (upper and lower) { fill((x0,0)--(x1,0)--(x1,upperparab(x1))--(x0,upperparab(x0))--cycle,color-nolight+1bp);}
}

trig_axes(-sqrt(6), 0, -1, 1);
trig_axes(-sqrt(6), 0, 1, 1);
trig_axes(-sqrt(6), 0, 0, sqrt(6/5));

label("$\sqrt{6}$",(sqrt(6)/2,0),S); label("$r$",(sqrt(6),0.2));
path ell = xscale(sqrt(6))*yscale(sqrt(6/5))*(Circle((0,0),1)));
