Then the equation of the ellipse is
\[\frac{x^2}{6} + \frac{5y^2}{6} = 1.\]Hence, at the point of tangency of the larger circle and ellipse, the value of $x$ is $\sqrt{6}.$  Let $(\sqrt{6}, y)$ be this point of tangency.  Substituting into the equation of the ellipse, we get
\[1 + \frac{3}{2} y^2 = 1,\]which gives $y = 0.$  The center of the smaller circle is at $\left(-\sqrt{6},0\right),$ so the equation of the smaller circle is given by
\[\left(x + \sqrt{6}\right)^2 + y^2 = r^2.\]In particular, the value $y$ is the same in each of the two equations above.  Hence,
\[1 + \frac{3}{2} y^2 \\
\Rightarrow \sqrt{6}^2 + y^2 = r^2.\]Then
\[(6 + \frac{3}{2} \cdot 0) = r^{2} \Leftrightarrow r = \cancel{2\sqrt{3}}.\][asy]
